---
// /mesonet/data/ (rebuild plan §4.2: "API docs, QC notes, bulk downloads";
// owner, 25 Oct 2026): where someone goes to get Mesonet data themselves.
// The one home for the API facts; the hub's "Get the data" section points
// here. Copy follows docs/VOICE.md. No counts in prose.
//
// Sources, each checked on 25 Oct 2026:
// - Free, and what the API serves: mco-website/_pages/mesonet/mesonet_api.md
//   and the OpenAPI info block ("free and easily accessed weather data").
// - Base URL, docs and spec: src/lib/mesonet.ts (mesonet2, the public AWS
//   API; CLAUDE.md). /api/docs, /api/v2/docs and /api/openapi.json each
//   answered 200.
// - Parameters (type default html; units us/si; tz default America/Denver;
//   elements partial names; stations comma list; level 0/1/2 and the
//   provisional column): /api/openapi.json, parameters of /latest/ and
//   /observations/daily/.
// - Epoch milliseconds in JSON (datetime 1792943100000) and local time with
//   offset in CSV ("2026-10-01 00:00:00-06:00"): live responses.
// - Display-string column names with units: live CSV header.
// - 30 s limit: mesonet-db-rds/terraform/apigateway.tf
//   (timeout_milliseconds = 30000); phase-0-report §6.1.
// - CORS: access-control-allow-origin: * on a cross-origin GET.
// - Station Dashboard download (CSV; Variables, Dates, Interval, Quality,
//   Units rows): mesonet-dashboard/web/partials/downloader/index.html, and
//   "Download data" in the live /dash/next/ page.
// - Station status CSV: src/pages/mesonet/status/stations.csv.ts and
//   csvRecord in src/lib/station-health.ts (last report, health columns).
// - Terms: the OpenAPI termsOfService is /about/agreement/.
//
// Left out, for staff review: data.climate.umt.edu/mesonet/ lists fgb/,
// misc/ and photos/ directories, but no page documents them as a public
// download, so this page does not offer them. The old API page's /photos/
// example is left out too: mesonet2's photo routes return 406 (D6).
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { API_BASE, API_ORIGIN, OPENAPI_URL } from '../../lib/mesonet';
import { tool } from '../../lib/tools';
import { url } from '../../lib/paths';

const API_DOCS = `${API_ORIGIN}/api/docs`;
const EXAMPLE_STATION = 'aceabsar';
// Both answered 200 with CSV on 25 Oct 2026.
const EXAMPLE_LATEST = `${API_BASE}/latest/?stations=${EXAMPLE_STATION}&type=csv`;
const EXAMPLE_DAILY = `${API_BASE}/observations/daily/?stations=${EXAMPLE_STATION}&elements=air_temp,ppt&start_time=2026-10-01&end_time=2026-10-07&type=csv`;
const dashboard = tool('Station Dashboard');
---
<Base short="Mesonet data"
      description="How to download Montana Mesonet data, through the API or without writing code, and how to cite it.">
  <!-- Copy drafted with AI assistance; needs MCO staff review before launch (UM AI disclosure). -->
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Montana Mesonet data</h1>
    <p>
      Everything the Mesonet records is free to download. Programmers can pull any station's
      full record from our API. Anyone who would rather not write code can save one station's
      readings from the <a href={dashboard.href}>{dashboard.name}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="api">The API</h2>
    <p>
      The Montana Mesonet API serves station details and observations as CSV or JSON. Every
      request starts from this address:
    </p>
    <p><code>{API_BASE}</code></p>
    <p>
      The <a href={API_DOCS}>interactive documentation</a> lists every endpoint and parameter, and
      it can run a request in the browser. The <a href={OPENAPI_URL}>OpenAPI description</a> is
      the same list in a form that code can read.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each request needs <code>type=csv</code> or <code>type=json</code>. Without it the API
      sends back an HTML table, which is handy for a quick look and awkward for anything else.
    </p>

    <h3>Examples</h3>
    <p>This asks for the latest readings at Absarokee (<code>{EXAMPLE_STATION}</code>) as CSV:</p>
    <pre tabindex="0"><code>{EXAMPLE_LATEST}</code></pre>
    <p>And this one asks for daily air temperature and precipitation at Absarokee over the first week of October 2026:</p>
    <pre tabindex="0"><code>{EXAMPLE_DAILY}</code></pre>

    <h3>Reading the results</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Column names carry their units, as in <code>Air Temperature @ 2 m [°F]</code>. US units
        are the default; <code>units=si</code> switches to metric.
      </li>
      <li>
        In JSON, <code>datetime</code> counts milliseconds since 1 January 1970 UTC. CSV writes
        local time with its offset from UTC, in the time zone named by <code>tz</code>, which
        defaults to Mountain time (<code>America/Denver</code>).
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>stations</code> takes one station ID, or several separated by commas. Every ID is in
        the <a href={url('mesonet/stations/')}>station directory</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>elements</code> takes element names such as <code>air_temp</code>. Part of a name
        works too, so <code>soil_vwc</code> brings back soil moisture at every depth. The{' '}
        <a href={url('mesonet/variables/')}>variables page</a> lists them all.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Limits</h3>
    <p>
      The API gives up on any request that takes longer than 30 seconds. For a long record, ask
      for fewer stations or a shorter stretch of dates at a time, and join the pieces afterwards.
    </p>
    <p>
      It answers requests from any website, so a web page can call it straight from the browser.
    </p>

    <h2 id="quality">Quality control</h2>
    <p>
      By default the API serves values that have passed all of our checks (<code>level=2</code>).
      Asking for <code>level=1</code> removes only values outside the sensor's range, and{' '}
      <code>level=0</code> returns the record exactly as each station sent it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A row marked <code>provisional</code> has not been through the daily checks yet, so its
      values can still change after the next morning's run.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each test and its thresholds are written up on the <a href={url('mesonet/qaqc/')}>quality
      control</a> page.
    </p>

    <h2 id="gis">GIS software</h2>
    <p>
      QGIS, ArcGIS, Python and R can all load the latest observations as map features through
      the <a href={url('mesonet/wfs/')}>Mesonet OGC API</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="without-code">Without writing code</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={dashboard.href}>{dashboard.name}</a> saves one station's readings as a CSV file,
      for whichever variables and dates are picked. Open a station and choose Download data.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href={url('mesonet/status/')}>station status</a> page has the whole station table as
      a CSV file, including when each station last reported.
    </p>

    <h2 id="terms">Terms and citation</h2>
    <p>
      Use of the data is covered by our <a href={url('about/agreement/')}>user agreement</a>.
      Research that uses Mesonet data should <a href={url('mesonet/') + '#mesonet-cite'}>cite it</a>{' '}
      in the form given on the Mesonet page.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
